domized, open label, dose– response clinical trial Jananee Muralidharan1, Cindy Romain1, Linda Chung2, Pedro Alcaraz2, Francisco Javier Martínez‑Noguera2, Mayoura Keophiphath3, Benjamin Lelouvier4, Patricia Ancel5, Benedicte Gaborit5 and Julien Cases1* Abstract Background Sinetrol ® Xpur is a polyphenolic ingredient rich in citrus flavonoids that has shown weight loss effects in previous studies. The dose dependent nature, gut microbial actions of this product has not been explored prev",ok,,Lacticaseibacillus paracasei K56; lactobacillus and; Bifidobacterium are; Lactobacillus gasseri SBT2055; Lactobacillus plantarum; Bifidobacterium longum; lactobacillus gas,SBT2055; BNR17,107 CFU/day; 109 CFU/day; 1010 CFU/day; 1011 CFU/ day; 109 CFU; 10 11 CFU/ day; 108 CFU; 107 CFU; 106 CFU/day,60 days; 12 weeks; 3 months; 60 day,74 subjects; 66 subjects; n = 13; n = 14; n = 12,pilot; randomized; placebo controlled; clinical trial,capsules; capsule; diet; exercise; milk,p = 0.004; p = 0.0007; p = 0.018; p = 0.003; p = 0.002; p = 0.01; p < 0.05; p < 0.01; p < 0.001; p > 0.05; p = 0.035,"30 kg/m2; 25%; 30%; decreases in percent body fat (p = 0; increases were observed in the placebo and VH_K56 groups compared with baseline values; changes in body fat mass, visceral fat area, weight, body mass index, waist circumference and hi; changes were made; increased prevalence of obesity has reached epidemic proportions, and presents a critical public h; increased mortality from type 2 diabetes (T2D), hypertension, and cardiovascular diseases, as well; changes in the gut microbial composition and function contribute to the pathophysi - ology of ob; reduces body weight gain, improves glucose tolerance in rodents through anti-inflammatory effect; reduced fat mass and serum lipid profile concurrently with down - regulation of lipogenic gene e; reduced abdomi - nal adiposity, waist and hip circumference or improved glucolipid metabolism fo; reduced body and fat mass and improved lipid metabolism [23]; improvement in the past two months; changes of dietary habits and the daily walking step numbers recorded by motion recorder was use; changes of physical activity; changes in body fat percent- age (PBF) and visceral fat area (VFA) from baseline to day 60","Nutrition & Metabolism (2023) 20:16 https://doi.org/10.1186/s12986-023-00739-y RESEARCH Effect of different doses of Lacticaseibacillus paracasei K56 on body fat and metabolic parameters in adult individuals with obesity: a pilot study Guzailinuer Kadeer1, Wanrui Fu1, Yaqi He1, Ying Feng1*, Wei‑Hsein Liu2, Wei‑Lian Hung2*, Haotian Feng2 and Wen Zhao2 Abstract Background Studies || The purpose of this study was to evaluate the effect of a novel probiotic strain Lacticaseibacillus paracasei K56 on body fat and metabolic biomarkers in adult individuals with obesity. || Methods 74 adult subjects with obesity (body mass index ≥ 30 kg/m2, or percent body fat > 25% for men, percent body fat > 30% for women) were randomized into 5 groups and supplemented with different doses of K56 (groups VL_K56, L_K56, H_K56, and VH_K56: K56 capsules, 2 × 107 CFU/day, 2 × 109 CFU/day, 2 × 1010 CFU/day, 2 × 1011 CFU/ day, respectively) or placebo (group Pla: plac || Results The results showed that the L_K56 group had significant decreases in percent body fat (p = 0.004), visceral fat area (p = 0.0007), total body fat mass (p = 0.018), trunk body fat mass (p = 0.003), waist circumference (p = 0.003), gly‑ cosylated hemoglobin(p = 0.002) at the end of the study compared with baseline. || There were non‑significant reduc‑ tions in Body weight and BMI in the L_K56, H_K56, VL_K56 groups, whereas increases were observed in the placebo and VH_K56 groups compared with baseline values. || In addition, K56 supplementation modulated gut microbiota characteristics and diversity indices in the L‑K56 group. || Conclusions The results suggest that supplementation with different doses of Lacticaseibacillus paracasei K56 has certain effect on reducing body fat and glycosylated hemoglobin, especially at a dose of 109 CFU/day.","Kadeer et al. Nutrition & Metabolism (2023) 20:16 https://doi.org/10.1186/s12986-023-00739-y RESEARCH Effect of different doses of Lacticaseibacillus paracasei K56 on body fat and metabolic parameters in adult individuals with obesity: a pilot study Guzailinuer Kadeer1, Wanrui Fu1, Yaqi He1, Ying Feng1*, Wei‑Hsein Liu2, Wei‑Lian Hung2*, Haotian Feng2 and Wen Zhao2 Abstract Background Studies have shown that probiotics have an effect on reducing body fat on a strain‑specific and dose– response bases. The purpose of this study was to evaluate the effect of a novel probiotic strain Lacticaseibacillus paracasei K56 on body fat and metabolic biomarkers in adult individuals with obesity. Methods 7",ok,
